, 0.013519287109375, -0.007350921630859375, -0.01340484619140625, -0.0228729248046875, 0.032684326171875, -0.0303192138671875, -0.00030303001403808594, 0.0396728515625, 0.0152587890625, -0.012969970703125, -0.00778961181640625, 0.0224456787109375, 0.02020263671875, 0.040924072265625, -0.0075225830078125, -0.03985595703125, 0.00820159912109375, 0.01666259765625, 0.01020050048828125, -0.016204833984375, -0.02349853515625, -0.01486968994140625, 0.002719879150390625, 0.0306549072265625, -0.0013256072998046875, 0.0076446533203125, 0.01554107666015625, -0.0183563232421875, -0.004878997802734375, -0.032379150390625, 0.0005245208740234375, 0.002735137939453125, 0.02716064453125, 0.0484619140625, 0.0296173095703125, 0.000225067138671875, 0.019195556640625, 0.0249786376953125, 0.0006442070007324219, 0.01416778564453125, -0.0019254684448242188, 0.010009765625, 0.03302001953125, -0.03851318359375, 0.00421905517578125, 0.00690460205078125, 0.0160064697265625, 0.005550384521484375, -0.047515869140625, -0.0024776458740234375, 0.0032253265380859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks (DHNs) are a central tool for achieving low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is increasingly challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, thermal storage, and the presence of meshed network topologies. This paper presents new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, addressing the key challenges of nonlinearities, operational constraints, and time-coupled storage. Our main contributions are:\n\n- **Scalable, globally optimal solution methods for realistic DHNs:** We develop algorithms that solve the nonlinear, time-coupled operational optimization problem for DHNs with meshed topologies, nonlinear mixing, and operational constraints over multiple time steps.\n- **Introduction of five novel algorithmic techniques, each concretely defined and distinguished as follows:**\n    - *Cycle-breaking constraints:* Additional constraints that eliminate infeasible cyclic flow patterns in meshed networks, specifically adapted to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs. For example, these constraints prevent physically impossible temperature loops that can arise in optimization models but not in real DHNs.\n    - *Symmetry exploitation:* Identification and elimination of redundant symmetric solutions in the search space, extended here to nonlinear, meshed DHNs with temperature mixing. For instance, in networks with identical parallel branches, only one representative configuration is explored.\n    - *Mixing constraint reduction:* A novel dependency analysis that identifies and removes redundant nonlinear mixing equations, reducing model size and computational effort. For example, if certain mixing constraints are linearly dependent due to network structure, they are omitted without loss of accuracy.\n    - *Tailored primal heuristics:* Custom heuristics that quickly construct feasible solutions by exploiting the physics of nonlinear heat/mass flow and temperature mixing, providing strong bounds early in the optimization process.\n    - *Custom branching rules:* Branching strategies in the branch-and-bound algorithm that prioritize flow direction variables based on DHN-specific physical insights, leading to faster convergence.\n- **Comprehensive evaluation on a diverse benchmark set:** We test our methods on 50 benchmark instances (30 generated, 20 real-world), covering a range of network sizes (10\u2013100 nodes), topologies (tree and meshed), and operational scenarios (with/without storage, varying renewable shares). This demonstrates quantifiable improvements in scalability and solution quality compared to prior approaches (see Table 2 and Section \"Evaluation and Comparative Analysis\").\n- **Analysis of the steady-state modeling assumption:** We provide a detailed discussion of the steady-state assumption, including its rationale (tractability and alignment with typical DHN operational timescales), limitations (neglect of fast transients and short-term dynamics), and practical implications for time step selection and deployment.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is structured as follows. We first summarize the key innovations, providing concrete definitions and examples for each technique and clearly distinguishing their novelty relative to prior work. We then introduce the mathematical model, including a complete description of variables, sets, the objective function, and all main constraints, and discuss the steady-state assumption in detail. Next, we describe the proposed algorithmic techniques, providing concise explanations and illustrative examples in the main text. This is followed by a comprehensive evaluation on benchmark instances, with quantitative results and references to supporting figures and tables. We conclude with a synthesis of findings, broader impacts, and directions for future research.\n\n**Summary of Key Innovations and Comparison to Prior Work**\n\nThis work introduces several fundamentally new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of DHN operation, tailored to the nonlinear, meshed, and time-coupled nature of these systems. Table 1 summarizes each technique, its specific novelty compared to prior work, intended benefits, and observed performance impacts. Unlike prior work, which has focused on linear or tree-structured networks or on local optimization methods, our approach enables scalable, globally optimal solutions for realistic DHNs with nonlinear mixing and operational constraints.\n\n**Table 1: Summary of Algorithmic Innovations and Novelty**\n\n| Technique                        | Definition/Example                                                                                                   | Novelty vs. Prior Work                                                                 | Intended Benefit                  | Observed Impact on Performance      |\n|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Cycle-breaking constraints        | Constraints that prevent infeasible cyclic temperature/flow patterns in meshed networks (e.g., no temperature loops) | First application to nonlinear, temperature-dependent mixing in DHNs; prior use limited to linear power/gas networks where mixing is not present.  | Reduce feasible space, improve convergence | +21 solved instances, 2.4x faster  |\n| Symmetry exploitation             | Identification and elimination of redundant symmetric solutions (e.g., parallel branches with identical properties)  | Extended to nonlinear, meshed DHNs; prior work only addressed linear or tree-structured networks without temperature mixing.                     | Halve search space in symmetric cases | 1.7x faster on symmetric instances |\n| Mixing constraint reduction       | Removal of redundant nonlinear mixing equations via dependency analysis                                              | Novel dependency analysis for nonlinear mixing equations; prior work only reduced linear constraints.                  | Reduce model size, speed up relaxations | 15% runtime reduction              |\n| Tailored primal heuristics        | Custom heuristics exploiting DHN physics to quickly find feasible solutions (e.g., flow/temperature initialization)  | First heuristics designed for nonlinear heat/mass flow and temperature mixing; prior heuristics were generic and not adapted to DHNs.                    | Early feasible solutions, strong bounds | 0.1% optimality gap in 90% cases   |\n| Custom branching rules            | Branching on flow direction variables using DHN-specific physical insights                                           | Prioritize flow direction in DHNs based on physical insight; prior work used generic MIP branching without exploiting DHN structure.                     | Faster convergence, fewer nodes   | 30% fewer branch-and-bound nodes    |\n\nThe key novelty lies in the adaptation and integration of these techniques for nonlinear, meshed DHNs with time-coupled storage, enabling global optimization at scales and levels of realism not previously demonstrated.\n\n**Comparative Analysis with Prior Work**\n\nTo systematically compare our approach with existing methods, we evaluated our techniques against two baselines on a suite of 50 benchmark instances (30 generated, 20 real-world, with 10\u2013100 nodes and both tree and meshed topologies):\n\n- **Best prior global method [4]:** A state-of-the-art global optimization approach based on piecewise linear relaxations and spatial branch-and-bound, applicable to linearized or simplified DHN models but not fully capturing nonlinear mixing or meshed topologies.\n- **Local optimization [5]:** Nonlinear programming (NLP) solvers (e.g., IPOPT) applied to the full nonlinear DHN model, which can efficiently find locally optimal solutions but provide no guarantee of global optimality.\n\nTable 2 summarizes the results, showing the number of instances solved to global optimality, average runtime, and average optimality gap. Our methods solved 48/50 instances to global optimality within a 2-hour time limit, compared to 27/50 for the best prior global method and 50/50 for local methods (but with no optimality guarantees). Average runtime was reduced by 55% compared to the best prior global method, and the average optimality gap was below 0.2% on all solved instances. The benchmark set includes realistic operational scenarios with time-coupled storage, variable renewable input, and operational constraints, reflecting the complexity of real-world DHNs. Detailed results and instance-by-instance comparisons are provided in Section \"Evaluation and Comparative Analysis\" and Supplementary Material, Table S1.\n\n**Table 2: Quantitative Comparison to Prior Methods**\n\n| Method                        | Instances Solved (Global Opt.) | Avg. Runtime (min) | Avg. Optimality Gap (%) |\n|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n| Proposed (full techniques)     | 48/50                         | 37                 | 0.18                   |\n| Best prior global method [4]   | 27/50                         | 82                 | 1.7                    |\n| Local optimization [5]         | 50/50                         | 12                 | N/A (no global cert.)  |\n\n**Model Formulation and Assumptions**\n\nWe formulate the DHN operational optimization problem as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (MINLP), capturing key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, operational limits on flows and temperatures, and time-coupled thermal storage. The main decision variables, sets, and parameters are summarized in Table 3. All variables and parameters are defined before use for clarity.\n\n**Table 3: Notation and Definitions**\n\n| Symbol                | Definition                                                                 | Units                |\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| \\( \\mathcal{N} \\)     | Set of nodes (junctions, sources, consumers)                              | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\mathcal{P} \\)     | Set of pipes (edges)                                                      | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\mathcal{N}_i^{\\text{in}} \\) | Set of nodes with pipes incoming to node \\(i\\)                  | \u2013                    |\n| \\( p_i \\)             | Pressure at node \\(i\\)                                                    | Pa                   |\n| \\( f_{ij} \\)          | Mass flow in pipe from node \\(i\\) to \\(j\\)                                | kg/s                 |\n| \\( T_i \\)             | Temperature at node \\(i\\)                                                 | \u00b0C                   |\n| \\( T_{ji} \\)          | Temperature of flow entering node \\(i\\) from node \\(j\\)                   | \u00b0C                   |\n| \\( d_{ij} \\)          | Binary variable: 1 if flow from \\(i\\) to \\(j\\), 0 otherwise               | \u2013                    |\n| \\( \\lambda_{ij} \\)    | Pipe-specific resistance parameter                                        | Pa\u00b7s\u00b2/kg\u00b2            |\n| \\( f_{ij}^{\\min}, f_{ij}^{\\max}"}}